    <body>
      <sec id="article-28577.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Riboflavin (vitamin B2) is a water-soluble, heat-stable vitamin essential for cellular energy metabolism. It serves as a precursor to flavin adenine dinucleotide and flavin mononucleotide, cofactors required for flavoproteins involved in the citric acid cycle, mitochondrial electron transport, redox reactions, and metabolism of other B vitamins. These processes support ATP production and cellular homeostasis. Riboflavin also has antioxidant functions and supports immune health as well as skin and mucosal integrity. Deficiency may cause mucocutaneous lesions, normocytic anemia, fatigue, and neuromuscular symptoms, and can lead to secondary niacin deficiency. At-risk populations include those with poor intake, malabsorption, chronic alcohol use, or genetic disorders affecting riboflavin metabolism. Evaluation includes clinical assessment and measurement of erythrocyte glutathione reductase activation coefficient. Management involves dietary optimization, supplementation, and interprofessional care to prevent complications and improve outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical features and risk factors associated with riboflavin deficiency in diverse patient populations.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate riboflavin deficiency from other micronutrient deficiencies based on clinical presentation and laboratory findings.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select&#x000a0;appropriate treatment strategies for the management of riboflavin deficiency.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Communicate patient-specific risk factors, diagnostic findings, and treatment plans clearly among interprofessional team members to support timely recognition and management of riboflavin deficiency.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28577&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28577">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Riboflavin (vitamin B2) is a water-soluble, heat-stable vitamin essential for cellular energy metabolism,&#x000a0;acting as a precursor&#x000a0;to&#x000a0;the coenzymes flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD). These cofactors are required by flavoproteins such as succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II), which participate in both the citric acid cycle and the mitochondrial electron transport chain, enabling adenosine triphosphate (ATP) production, maintaining redox balance, and supporting antioxidant defenses. Beyond energy metabolism, riboflavin contributes to immune function and to maintaining the health of skin, hair, and mucous membranes. Deficiency disrupts these pathways, leading to fatigue, normocytic anemia, neuromuscular symptoms, and secondary impairments in iron and other B-vitamin metabolism, including the activation of niacin (vitamin B3) and pyridoxine (vitamin B6).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Because humans cannot synthesize riboflavin, adequate dietary intake is necessary, and absorption depends on normal gastrointestinal function. Excess riboflavin is excreted in urine, producing the characteristic yellow color that indicates adequate intake. Clinical deficiency is characterized by mucocutaneous manifestations (cheilosis, angular stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis) and anemia; in severe or prolonged cases, it may lead to neurologic or ocular metabolic abnormalities, reduced physical performance, and developmental problems.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Emerging evidence also links riboflavin deficiency to psoriasis, with decreased intake associated with a higher risk and upregulated riboflavin metabolism observed in psoriatic skin, suggesting a protective role for adequate riboflavin status, particularly in individuals&#x000a0;older than 40 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>This article reviews the etiology, epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, evaluation, management, complications, and prognosis of riboflavin deficiency. The article also highlights&#x000a0;the roles of patient education and interprofessional healthcare team collaboration in optimizing patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Riboflavin deficiency results from inadequate dietary intake, malabsorption, increased physiological demands, certain medications, chronic alcohol use, and genetic disorders affecting riboflavin transport or metabolism.</p>
        <p>Dietary insufficiency is the most common cause globally. In Western diets, adequate riboflavin intake relies heavily on dairy products, meat, eggs, and fish, with dark green leafy vegetables and fortified cereals serving as additional sources. Low consumption of these foods, particularly in low-income countries and rural settings, leads to deficiency. Even in high-income countries, inadequate intake occurs among older adults and adolescents despite food availability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Chronic alcohol consumption&#x000a0;impairs riboflavin status through multiple mechanisms. Ethanol blocks intestinal riboflavin receptors&#x000a0;and inhibits both intestinal absorption and renal reabsorption by reducing expression of riboflavin transporters (RFVT-1 and RFVT-3) at the transcriptional level.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Individuals with alcohol use disorder are also at risk of reduced riboflavin intake due to nutritionally inadequate diets, making chronic alcohol use a significant risk factor for deficiency.</p>
        <p>Increased physiological demands during pregnancy, lactation, adolescence, and aging place these populations at heightened risk for riboflavin deficiency. When a marginal or subclinical deficiency is present, superimposed stressors such as infection, inflammation, or increased physical activity may precipitate clinically significant manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Medical conditions and treatments&#x000a0;that contribute to deficiency include respiratory infections, certain chronic diseases, malabsorption, hemodialysis, and&#x000a0;neonatal&#x000a0;phototherapy for hyperbilirubinemia, which degrades the photosensitive vitamin.&#x000a0;Certain medications, including oral contraceptives, and hormonal factors have been associated with alterations in riboflavin metabolism; however, current evidence does not support a direct causal role in the development of clinically significant deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/31218">Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin)</ext-link>," for more information.</p>
        <p>Genetic disorders include riboflavin transporter deficiency (RTD), which impairs cellular uptake; multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency (MADD) and related disorders affecting mitochondrial fatty acid &#x003b2;-oxidation; and Charcot-Marie-Tooth X-linked disease type 4. These inborn errors can lead to riboflavin deficiency despite adequate dietary intake. Riboflavin deficiency is also associated with hereditary motor neuropathies, such as RTD, also known as Brown-Vialette-Van Laere or Fazio-Londe syndromes. RTD is a progressive, inherited childhood-onset neuropathy/neuronopathy that can mimic other distal motor neuropathies, but is treatable with high-dose riboflavin when detected early. If untreated, RTD may cause severe motor and sensory disability and fatal respiratory failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Other transporter-mediated deficiencies have been linked to spermatozoa immotility and male infertility in rodents, but evidence in humans is lacking.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Riboflavin deficiency occurs globally, with prevalence varying substantially by geographic region, population group, and the biochemical threshold used to define the condition. Severe clinical deficiency (ariboflavinosis), characterized by cheilosis, angular stomatitis, glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, and severe anemia with erythroid hypoplasia, is most often diagnosed in low-income countries. In contrast, subclinical riboflavin deficiency appears to be far more prevalent worldwide, including in high-income countries.&#x000a0;Using the commonly accepted biochemical cutoff for erythrocyte glutathione reductase activation coefficient (EGRac) &#x02265;1.40, deficiency rates range from approximately 40% in some high-income countries to more than 70% in&#x000a0;many middle- and low-income countries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Growing evidence indicates that low or deficient riboflavin status across the life course contributes to adverse health outcomes, including anemia and hypertension, with potential implications for global disease burden.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>In high-income countries, subclinical riboflavin deficiency is more common than generally appreciated. Among Canadian women of reproductive age, 40% demonstrate biochemical deficiency (EGRac &#x02265;1.40).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;In&#x000a0;another study of riboflavin status in similar women, about half in Ireland and&#x000a0;the United Kingdom&#x000a0;were found to have&#x000a0;riboflavin deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Milk and dairy products are the best dietary sources of riboflavin, and people in countries with low consumption of these foods face the highest risk of deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;In the U.S., the true prevalence remains uncertain because riboflavin biomarkers are not routinely measured in national nutrition surveys and population studies.&#x000a0;However, when dairy intake is adequate, deficiency appears less&#x000a0;prevalent than in Europe.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Fortified breakfast cereals are an important source of micronutrients for U.S. children, which may partly explain why deficiency data differ from European populations, where food fortification practices vary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Deficiency rates are substantially higher in low- and middle-income countries. Among Malaysian women of reproductive age, 71%&#x000a0;demonstrated&#x000a0;biochemical riboflavin deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Studies from India and other regions with developing economies&#x000a0;show a very high prevalence of biochemical deficiency, especially among women and children. Overt clinical manifestations of riboflavin deficiency remain largely confined to these low-income settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Deficiency most commonly results from inadequate dietary intake and is particularly prevalent in South Asia and in eastern and southern Africa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Certain populations are consistently at elevated risk across regions. Women of reproductive age and pregnant women have particularly high rates of suboptimal riboflavin status. In Western countries, adolescents and older adults may also be affected despite access to riboflavin-rich foods. Individuals living in rural settings appear to be at increased risk globally.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>The actual global burden of riboflavin deficiency is likely underestimated. Riboflavin status is poorly monitored worldwide, although the United Kingdom and Ireland conduct routine testing more frequently&#x000a0;than many other countries. Fewer nations use precise biochemical assessments, such as EGRac or urinary riboflavin excretion, limiting accurate surveillance. Discrepancies between dietary intake estimates and biochemical markers suggest that current riboflavin intake recommendations may be underestimated&#x000a0;or that biochemical thresholds for deficiency may require revision. Emerging food insecurity in high-income countries, including the United States, further suggests that riboflavin deficiency may be significantly underrecognized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite its high prevalence, the functional significance of suboptimal riboflavin status across populations, particularly its contribution to&#x000a0;anemia, remains poorly understood. Understanding the pathophysiologic effects of riboflavin deficiency is essential for linking biochemical deficiency&#x000a0;with clinical and subclinical disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Riboflavin is an essential water-soluble vitamin that functions through its active cofactors, FMN and FAD, which support mitochondrial energy production, antioxidant defense, redox homeostasis, and the metabolism of iron and other B vitamins. Depletion of these flavoprotein cofactors in riboflavin deficiency disrupts cellular metabolism, providing&#x000a0;a mechanistic basis for the hematologic, metabolic, and oxidative manifestations of the disease.</p>
        <p>Riboflavin is critical for the function of flavoproteins such as succinate dehydrogenase (SDH; Complex II), which participates in both the citric acid cycle and the mitochondrial electron transport chain. SDH depends on FAD to catalyze the oxidation of succinate to fumarate and to transfer electrons for ATP production. In riboflavin deficiency, SDH activity is impaired, reducing ATP production and leading to the accumulation of metabolic intermediates that contribute to the hematologic, metabolic, and oxidative manifestations of the condition. In severe or genetic forms of deficiency, such as RTD, the disorder&#x000a0;may mimic primary mitochondrial disease, presenting with neuromuscular symptoms, hypotonia, developmental delay, and characteristic muscle biopsy findings. High-dose riboflavin supplementation can restore SDH activity and reverse these biochemical and clinical abnormalities, underscoring riboflavin's central role in cellular energy metabolism.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Contributing Factors</bold> Aside from inadequate dietary intake, riboflavin deficiency can result from multiple conditions, including alcohol use disorder, diabetes mellitus, hepatic disease, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, gastrointestinal disorders, and biliary obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Chronic alcohol consumption impairs riboflavin absorption and renal reabsorption by altering the expression of riboflavin transporters.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Hormonal oral contraceptives may increase urinary excretion of intermediates in the kynurenine pathway, which depends on FAD and pyridoxal 5&#x02032;-phosphate, thereby reducing riboflavin and pyridoxine levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r18">[18]</xref> Genetic causes include RTDs due to mutations in the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">SLC52A2</italic> or <italic toggle="yes">SLC52A3</italic> genes<bold>, </bold>which encode RFVT2 and RFVT3, respectively, impairing&#x000a0;intestinal absorption and systemic distribution of riboflavin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cellular and Metabolic Effects</bold> Riboflavin deficiency depletes the cofactors FMN and FAD, which are essential for mitochondrial energy production, antioxidant defense, and the metabolism of other B vitamins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;This disruption leads to mitochondrial dysfunction, impaired fatty acid &#x003b2;-oxidation, reduced ATP production, and the accumulation of toxic metabolic intermediates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Oxidative stress, endoplasmic reticulum stress, and activation of the CHOP pathway promote apoptosis in affected cells. Lipid metabolism is also disrupted, leading to lipid accumulation and lipotoxicity in high-energy-demand tissues, such as the liver, retina, and nervous system.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Impact on Micronutrient Metabolism</bold>Riboflavin deficiency impairs folate recycling by reducing FAD-dependent methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) activity, disrupts vitamin B6 and niacin metabolism, and decreases iron mobilization from stores, collectively contributing to anemia even in individuals with adequate dietary iron intake.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Oxidative Stress and Neurophysiologic Effects</bold>Deficiency increases reactive oxygen species, contributing to inflammation and neurodegenerative disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Riboflavin deficiency&#x000a0;also impairs retinal function by altering the retinal pigment epithelium and lipid metabolism, both of which are critical for photoreceptor maintenance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Chronic deficiency can accelerate degenerative processes and contribute to neurodegenerative disease progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic and Tissue-Level Effects</bold>Riboflavin deficiency&#x000a0;affects multiple organ systems:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Skeletal and connective tissue: Decreased bone metabolism and impaired collagen cross-linking reduce tissue resilience in the skin, hair, and cornea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r23">[23]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiovascular: Elevated homocysteine levels contribute to cardiovascular and neuroinflammatory diseases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r24">[24]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gastrointestinal: Esophageal epithelial atrophy&#x000a0;may contribute to esophageal cancer by altering the esophageal microbiota.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s6" sec-type="Toxicokinetics">
        <title>Toxicokinetics</title>
        <p>Riboflavin supplementation is generally considered safe in its nonphotoactivated form. As a water-soluble vitamin, excess riboflavin is not stored and is rapidly eliminated via renal excretion&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;distributed&#x000a0;into the extracellular fluid. However, riboflavin exhibits altered biologic behavior upon exposure to UV-A radiation, becoming photoactivated and capable of damaging DNA and cellular structures. This exposure-dependent transformation is clinically relevant, as controlled UV-A activation is intentionally used in ophthalmologic protocols, whereas inadvertent photoactivation represents a distinct exposure pathway with different toxicologic implications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r25">[25]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Riboflavin deficiency primarily manifests as mucocutaneous lesions and hematologic abnormalities, though severe deficiency is rare in middle- and high-income regions. The classic presentation includes angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, and normocytic, normochromic anemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Subclinical deficiency is far more common than overt deficiency and is usually identified by biochemical abnormalities (eg, EGRac &#x02265; 1.40), with or without mild anemia, and in the absence of classic mucocutaneous manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Relevant clinical history&#x000a0;may include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Sore throat and oral discomfort</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Photophobia&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Exercise intolerance or proximal muscle weakness (particularly in genetic forms)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptoms of anemia, such as fatigue and weakness</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dietary history revealing low intake of dairy products, meat, or fish&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic alcohol use, which blocks intestinal riboflavin receptors&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use of oral contraceptives</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recent respiratory infections or chronic&#x000a0;illness</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>&#x000a0;Physical examination findings&#x000a0;include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Oropharyngeal manifestations: Angular stomatitis&#x000a0;(cracks or fissures at the corners of the mouth), glossitis&#x000a0;(inflamed, smooth, magenta-colored tongue), and cheilosis (dryness, scaling, fissuring, and cracking of the lips)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dermatologic findings:&#x000a0;Seborrhea-like dermatitis&#x000a0;affecting the face, trunk, and scrotum&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ophthalmologic findings:&#x000a0;Corneal vascularization&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hematologic manifestations: Signs of anemia (pallor, tachycardia), in severe cases, such as RTD, bone marrow aplasia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Evaluation of suspected riboflavin deficiency requires a comprehensive dietary and risk-factor assessment, a physical examination for characteristic mucocutaneous lesions, and biochemical testing using the EGRac, the most reliable method for assessing riboflavin status.</p>
        <p>EGRac values &#x0003e;1.4 indicate deficiency, values of 1.2&#x02013;1.4 indicate subnormal or low status, and values of &#x0003c;1.2 are acceptable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;This functional biomarker measures the in vitro stimulation of erythrocyte glutathione reductase by FAD, derived from riboflavin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;In a therapeutic trial&#x000a0;in a patient with suspected riboflavin deficiency, EGRac measurements before and after supplementation provide the most reliable assessment of deficiency and response to treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Alternative biomarkers include urinary riboflavin excretion (&#x0003c;27 mcg/g creatinine suggests deficiency, though this measure is less reliable in catabolic states or with certain drugs, such as antibiotics and phenothiazines) and erythrocyte riboflavin levels (&#x0003c;10 mcg/dL red cells or &#x0003c;170 nmol/L indicates deficiency). In populations with a high prevalence of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency, erythrocyte pyridoxamine phosphate oxidase activity can serve as an alternative biomarker&#x000a0;because EGRac is insensitive to poor riboflavin status in G6PD-deficient individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Hemoglobin and iron status (including ferritin and soluble transferrin receptor) should be assessed because of the strong association between riboflavin deficiency and anemia. Women with biochemical riboflavin deficiency (EGRac &#x02265;1.40) have approximately twice the risk of anemia as those with adequate riboflavin status.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Assessment of other B vitamins (folate, vitamin B6, and vitamin B12) is also warranted, as riboflavin is essential for their metabolism, and individuals with dietary riboflavin deficiency often have concurrent B-vitamin deficiencies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Riboflavin status may help assess elevated homocysteine levels, particularly in individuals with the MTHFR C677T TT genotype and low folate status, because MTHFR is a FAD-dependent enzyme involved in homocysteine remethylation. The effect of riboflavin is most pronounced in this subgroup, in whom adequate riboflavin status is associated with significantly lower homocysteine concentrations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r33">[33]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Riboflavin deficiency is treated with oral riboflavin supplementation, with dosing tailored to the underlying cause. Physiologic replacement doses (2-4 mg/day) are sufficient for dietary deficiency, whereas high-dose therapy (10-50 mg/kg/day or higher) is required for genetic disorders affecting riboflavin metabolism.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Dietary Insufficiency and Acquired Deficiency</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>For deficiency due to inadequate intake, chronic alcohol use, or increased physiological demand, supplementation with 2 to 4 mg/day for 8 weeks effectively corrects the biochemical deficiency and improves hematologic status.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>In a study of moderately riboflavin-deficient young women (EGRac &#x0003e;1.40), daily supplementation with either 2 or 4 mg of riboflavin significantly improved riboflavin status, with hemoglobin concentrations increasing in proportion to the amount of riboflavin repletion. The greatest hematologic improvement was observed among those with the most severe baseline deficiency (EGRac &#x0003e;1.65).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r34">[34]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Duration and Monitoring</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Supplementation should continue until biochemical markers normalize (EGRac &#x0003c;1.40) and clinical signs resolve.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Biochemical depletion occurs within days, whereas repletion typically requires several weeks.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>EGRac and hemoglobin levels should be monitored to assess response to treatment.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Special Populations</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pregnant and lactating women have increased riboflavin requirements. The USDA/NIH recommended dietary allowances are 1.4 mg/day during pregnancy and 1.6 mg/day during lactation. The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) recommends a population reference intake above the minimum requirement: approximately 1.9 mg/day during pregnancy and 2 mg/day during lactation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r35">[35]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Individuals with chronic alcohol use disorder may require higher doses and longer treatment because of impaired absorption and reabsorption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r28">[28]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Genetic Disorders Affecting Riboflavin Metabolism</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Conditions&#x000a0;such as RTD and MADD require high-dose riboflavin (10-50 mg/kg/day), which can be lifesaving. Lifelong treatment is indicated unless pathogenic variants are excluded by genetic testing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r36">[36]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Supplement Forms and Dietary Sources of Riboflavin</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Riboflavin tablets are available in 25, 50, and 100 mg doses.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oral supplementation is preferred; injections are reserved for patients who cannot tolerate oral therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dietary sources are the best way to prevent deficiency; these include dairy products, meat, eggs, mushrooms, dark green leafy vegetables, and fortified grains and cereals. Excess riboflavin beyond metabolic needs is typically excreted in urine.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Recommended Daily Allowances (USDA/NIH)</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Infants: 0 to&#x000a0;6 months, 0.4 mg/day; 7 to&#x000a0;12 months, 0.3 mg/day</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Children: 1 to 3 years, 0.5 mg/day; 4 to 9 years, 0.9 mg/day</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adolescents: girls 10&#x000a0;to 17 years, 1 mg/day; boys 10&#x000a0;to 17 years, 1.3 mg/day</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adults: men, 1.3 mg/day; women (nonpregnant), 1.1 mg/day; pregnant, 1.4 mg/day; lactating, 1.6 mg/day; older adults, 1.1 to 1.3 mg/day.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r39">[39]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The clinical presentation of angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, and normocytic, normochromic anemia suggests riboflavin deficiency; however, several other conditions can produce similar clinical manifestations.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Other B-vitamin deficiencies,</bold> including niacin deficiency (pellagra), pyridoxine (vitamin B6) deficiency, and folate or vitamin B12 deficiency, are primary differential considerations. Niacin deficiency typically presents with dermatitis on sun-exposed areas, glossitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) deficiency causes glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis, and angular stomatitis, similar to those seen in&#x000a0;riboflavin deficiency. Individuals with folate and vitamin B12 deficiency may develop glossitis and anemia, although the anemia is typically macrocytic rather than normocytic. These B-vitamin deficiencies often coexist because riboflavin is required for the metabolism of folate, vitamin B6, and niacin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Iron deficiency anemia</bold>&#x000a0;may present with angular stomatitis, glossitis, and normocytic anemia before progressing to microcytic anemia. Iron deficiency affects approximately 14% of US adults and is particularly common in menstruating women (38% with iron deficiency without anemia, 13% with iron-deficiency anemia). Riboflavin deficiency impairs iron mobilization from stores, so these conditions frequently overlap.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r41">[41]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Zinc deficiency&#x000a0;</bold>produces glossitis, angular stomatitis, alopecia, nail dystrophy, and dermatitis.&#x000a0;This deficiency&#x000a0;occurs in patients with inflammatory bowel disease and other malabsorption syndromes, as well as in those with alcohol use disorder&#x02014;the same populations at risk for riboflavin deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r42">[42]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Seborrheic dermatitis and atopic dermatitis</bold> can mimic the dermatologic findings of riboflavin deficiency. Seborrheic dermatitis affects the face, scalp, and trunk, presenting with erythema and scaling. In young infants, seborrheic dermatitis and atopic dermatitis may overlap.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r43">[43]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Allergic or irritant contact cheilitis</bold>&#x000a0;presents with involvement of the vermilion border&#x000a0;and adjacent perioral skin, often from fragrances in oral hygiene products, cosmetics, or lip balms. Angular cheilitis specifically can result from contact allergens, though it more commonly has an infectious etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r44">[44]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Oral candidiasis</bold>&#x000a0;produces angular cheilitis (erythematous patches with scaling and fissuring at the labial commissures bilaterally), erythematous glossitis, and pseudomembranous lesions. Risk factors include immunosuppression, corticosteroid use, antibiotic use, dentures, and salivary gland hypofunction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r45">[45]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In addition to conditions that mimic the signs and symptoms of riboflavin deficiency, clinicians should consider underlying disorders that predispose patients to low riboflavin status, particularly when biochemical abnormalities are confirmed. These disorders include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Chronic alcohol use disorder</bold>&#x000a0;causes multiple coexisting micronutrient deficiencies. Ethanol blocks intestinal riboflavin receptors and impairs the absorption of thiamine, folate, vitamin C, selenium, iron, and zinc. The clinical presentation may reflect combined deficiencies rather than an isolated riboflavin deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r46">[46]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Malabsorption syndromes,</bold> including<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, and prior bariatric procedures that bypass the duodenum and proximal jejunum, cause multiple micronutrient deficiencies. Celiac disease should be considered in unexplained malabsorption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r47">[47]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Malignancy with cachexia</bold> produces severe weight loss and wasting, with secondary nutritional deficiencies affecting 18% to 82% of cancer patients, depending on the tumor type.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r41">[41]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Eating disorders</bold>&#x000a0;lead to malnutrition and multiple micronutrient deficiencies. Signs and symptoms include weight loss, abnormal vital signs (bradycardia, orthostatic changes, hypothermia), cachexia, lanugo, salivary gland enlargement, and dental enamel erosions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of riboflavin deficiency varies widely and depends on the underlying etiology, the duration of the deficiency, and the timeliness of recognition and treatment.</p>
        <p>For individuals with a deficiency due to inadequate dietary intake, such as diets lacking dairy, meat, and vegetables, restrictive diets, or food insecurity, the prognosis is excellent. Biochemical abnormalities and clinical manifestations typically resolve within weeks with appropriate oral supplementation and dietary correction, with no long-term sequelae when treatment is prompt.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r49">[49]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients with increased physiological requirements, including those with pregnancy and lactation, also have a favorable prognosis, provided the deficiency is identified and intake is adjusted to meet these increased requirements. Correction of riboflavin status in these populations is associated with improved hematologic indices and metabolic function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>In cases of acquired malabsorption, such as&#x000a0;inflammatory bowel disease, celiac disease, bariatric surgery, or chronic alcohol use, the prognosis depends on the&#x000a0;underlying condition and adherence to long-term supplementation. Ongoing monitoring&#x000a0;is usually&#x000a0;required, and persistent malabsorption places&#x000a0;patients at risk for recurrent deficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>The prognosis varies markedly in genetic disorders affecting riboflavin transport or metabolism, including RTD and MADD. In these conditions, prognosis depends heavily on early recognition and initiation of high-dose riboflavin therapy. Prompt treatment can be lifesaving and may halt or reverse neurologic and systemic manifestations, whereas delayed diagnosis is associated with progressive neurologic impairment, disability, or death. Lifelong supplementation is typically required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r50">[50]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, riboflavin deficiency is highly treatable, and outcomes are generally favorable when the underlying cause is identified and therapy is tailored accordingly. Poor outcomes are uncommon and are primarily associated with delayed diagnosis, persistent malabsorption, or untreated genetic disease.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Untreated riboflavin deficiency can lead to systemic complications beyond the presenting signs and symptoms, including anemia, hypertension, disruption of other B-vitamin metabolism, retinal and neurological dysfunction, and cellular oxidative damage.&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;severity and reversibility&#x000a0;of complications depend on the duration and degree of deficiency, highlighting the importance of&#x000a0;early detection and supplementation, particularly to prevent permanent visual and neurological damage.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hematologic complications:</bold> Riboflavin deficiency doubles the risk of anemia in women and impairs iron metabolism by reducing absorption, mobilization, and utilization, often resulting in normocytic, normochromic anemia that is resistant to iron supplementation alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cardiovascular effects:</bold>&#x000a0;Riboflavin deficiency has been associated with an increased risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and has been reported to affect cardiovascular mortality, lipid metabolism, homocysteine regulation, and blood pressure control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Low riboflavin status may contribute to elevated blood pressure by inducing endothelial dysfunction and increasing vascular oxidative stress.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;At the mechanistic level, riboflavin deficiency impairs flavoprotein-dependent antioxidant defenses, leading to oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, and endoplasmic reticulum stress. These disturbances promote apoptosis, endothelial injury, and vascular inflammation, thereby facilitating atherogenesis and the progression of cardiovascular pathology. Animal studies support these findings, demonstrating the accumulation of cardiac injury biomarkers, histopathologic changes in the myocardium, and reduced mitochondrial membrane potential in riboflavin-deficient states.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Metabolic effects:</bold> Riboflavin is essential for the metabolism of folate, vitamin B6, and niacin; deficiency can lead to secondary vitamin deficiencies and exacerbate overall nutritional deficits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual and neurological complications:</bold> Prolonged deficiency can cause irreversible retinal dysfunction and neurological deterioration in genetic disorders such as RTD and MADD.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Early supplementation may prevent or reverse these outcomes,&#x000a0;whereas delayed treatment may lead to permanent impairment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cellular consequences:</bold> Riboflavin deficiency impairs mitochondrial function, increases oxidative stress, disrupts lipid metabolism, and induces apoptosis, particularly affecting high-energy tissues such as the liver, retina, and nervous system.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28577.r52">[52]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Preventing riboflavin deficiency focuses on ensuring adequate dietary intake and addressing risk factors. Patients should be encouraged to&#x000a0;consume riboflavin-rich foods, including dairy products, eggs, lean meats, fortified grains, dark green leafy vegetables, and mushrooms. Clinicians should consider supplementation for high-risk groups, including individuals with restrictive diets, malabsorption syndromes, alcohol use disorder, or increased physiological demands, such as pregnancy and lactation.</p>
        <p>Patient education should emphasize recognition of&#x000a0;early signs of deficiency, including lip or tongue inflammation, cracks at the corners of the mouth, eye sensitivity, or unexplained fatigue, and the need for consistent daily adherence to prescribed supplementation, particularly in patients with chronic malabsorption or genetic disorders&#x000a0;such as RTD or MADD. Patients should also understand that biochemical deficiency can occur before overt symptoms appear. Follow-up laboratory monitoring, including assessment of eGFR and hemoglobin, is recommended to confirm repletion and prevent recurrence. In genetic disorders, early and ongoing high-dose riboflavin therapy is essential to prevent potentially irreversible complications, including neurologic and visual&#x000a0;impairment.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Riboflavin deficiency is generally preventable through a varied diet that includes dairy products, lean meats, eggs, mushrooms, dark green leafy vegetables, and fortified cereals and grains. An interprofessional healthcare team, including the primary care clinician, nurse practitioners or physician assistants, nurses, pharmacists, and registered dietitians, can collaborate to optimize patient education and monitoring.</p>
        <p>Clinicians recognize and diagnose riboflavin deficiency based on clinical features, risk factors, and laboratory findings; counsel patients on diet and supplementation; and adjust care when medications interfere with riboflavin metabolism.&#x000a0;Pharmacists advise on appropriate supplementation, while dietitians monitor dietary intake and ensure the inclusion of riboflavin-rich foods.&#x000a0;Nurses track patient progress, document changes in clinical or biochemical status during follow-up visits, and reinforce nutritional recommendations.&#x000a0;Through interprofessional collaboration, the healthcare team&#x000a0;helps prevent riboflavin deficiency, facilitates early diagnosis, and supports effective management to optimize patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28577.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
